  KEY INSIGHT: The Cayley transform Q maps the circle (where Jones polynomial
  is typically evaluated) to the imaginary axis. Knot invariants at imaginary
  arguments probe a different 'rapidity regime' of the knot.

  The Jones polynomial at t = e^{2*pi*i/n} connects to quantum groups at
  q = e^{2*pi*i/n} (Witten-Reshetikhin-Turaev invariants).
  Composing with Q: the quantum group parameter becomes purely imaginary,
  which corresponds to the 'split real form' of the quantum group.


========================================================================
SECTION 5: HOPF FIBRATION THROUGH Q
========================================================================

The Hopf fibration: S^3 -> S^2 with fiber S^1.

S^3 = {(z_1, z_2) in C^2 : |z_1|^2 + |z_2|^2 = 1}
Hopf map: h(z_1, z_2) = (2*Re(z_1*conj(z_2)), 2*Im(z_1*conj(z_2)), |z_1|^2-|z_2|^2)
This maps to S^2 in R^3. The fiber over each point is a great circle in S^3.

Alternative: h(z_1, z_2) = z_1/z_2 in CP^1 = S^2 (Riemann sphere).
The Hopf invariant = 1 (the linking number of any two fibers).

APPLYING Q TO THE HOPF MAP:

  For (z_1, z_2) on S^3, the ratio w = z_1/z_2 is on the Riemann sphere.
  Apply Q to w: Q(w) = (1+w)/(1-w) = (z_2+z_1)/(z_2-z_1).

  Q(z_1/z_2) = (z_1 + z_2)/(z_2 - z_1)

  This is a CAYLEY-TRANSFORMED Hopf map. It maps S^3 -> C_inf via:
    h_Q(z_1, z_2) = (z_1 + z_2) / (z_2 - z_1)

  Special fibers:
    h_Q = 0  when z_1 + z_2 = 0, i.e., z_2 = -z_1 => original w = -1
    h_Q = inf when z_2 = z_1 => original w = 1
    h_Q = 1  when z_1 + z_2 = z_2 - z_1 => 2*z_1 = 0 => w = 0 (south pole)
    h_Q = -1 when z_1 + z_2 = -(z_2 - z_1) => 2*z_2 = 0 => w = inf (north pole)

  FIBER PARAMETRIZATION:
  The fiber over w_0 in the Hopf map: (z_1, z_2) = (e^{it}*cos(a), e^{it}*sin(a)*e^{ip})
  where w_0 = cot(a)*e^{-ip}, t parametrizes the fiber circle.

  For w_0 on the equator (|w_0| = 1), write w_0 = e^{ip}:
    a = pi/4, so |z_1| = |z_2| = 1/sqrt(2)
    Q(w_0) = Q(e^{ip}) = i*cot(p/2)

  The EQUATOR of S^2 maps to the IMAGINARY AXIS under Q!
  This is the rapidity axis for the equatorial fibers.

  Sample equatorial fibers (w_0 = e^{ip}):
     p/(2pi)                  w_0    Q(w_0)=i*cot(p/2)        |Q|
  ------------------------------------------------------------
      0.0833    +0.8660+0.5000i    -0.0000+3.7321i     3.7321
      0.1667    +0.5000+0.8660i    -0.0000+1.7321i     1.7321
      0.2500    +0.0000+1.0000i    -0.0000+1.0000i     1.0000
      0.3333    -0.5000+0.8660i    +0.0000+0.5774i     0.5774
      0.4167    -0.8660+0.5000i    +0.0000+0.2679i     0.2679
      0.5000    -1.0000+0.0000i    -0.0000+0.0000i     0.0000
      0.5833    -0.8660-0.5000i    +0.0000-0.2679i     0.2679
      0.6667    -0.5000-0.8660i    -0.0000-0.5774i     0.5774
      0.7500    -0.0000-1.0000i    +0.0000-1.0000i     1.0000
      0.8333    +0.5000-0.8660i    -0.0000-1.7321i     1.7321
      0.9167    +0.8660-0.5000i    +0.0000-3.7321i     3.7321

  HOPF INVARIANT AND RAPIDITY:
  The Hopf invariant = integral_{S^3} alpha ^ d(alpha) where alpha = h*(vol_{S^2})
  = linking number of any two fibers = 1.

  rapidity(1) = arctanh(1) = INFINITY.
  But 1 is a very specific infinity: Q(1) = inf, and Q^{-1}(inf) = 1.

  Better: use f(n) = (n+1)/n. f(1) = 2, rapidity(f(1)) = arctanh(2) -- complex!
  f(1) = 2 => rapidity_from_integer(2) = ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2
  = (0.5493061443340549+1.5707963267948966j)

  The Hopf invariant 1, through f, becomes the same rapidity as the sphere (chi=2)!
  This is NOT a coincidence: the Hopf fibration is deeply connected to S^2.

  LINKING IN RAPIDITY:
  Two linked circles have linking number +/- 1.
  Two unlinked circles have linking number 0.
  Q(linking number = 0) = Q(0) = 1 (neutral)
  Q(linking number = 1) = Q(1) = inf
  Q(linking number = -1) = Q(-1) = 0

  Linking 0 -> Q = 1 (the boundary between 'linked' and 'anti-linked')
  Linking +1 -> Q = infinity (maximally linked)
  Linking -1 -> Q = 0 (maximally anti-linked)

  Rapidity: lk=0 -> 0 (neutral), lk=+1 -> +inf, lk=-1 -> -inf
  The rapidity of linking number is arctanh(lk), which diverges at +/-1.
  This is consistent with the TOPOLOGICAL NATURE of linking: you cannot
  continuously interpolate between linked and unlinked, just as you cannot
  reach rapidity infinity at finite boost.


========================================================================
SECTION 6: BETTI NUMBERS OF TOURNAMENT COMPLEXES -> RAPIDITY
========================================================================

From tournament path homology (established results):
  beta_0 >= 1 (from n=1)
  beta_1 >= 0 (from n=3)
  beta_2 = 0 ALWAYS (deepest invariant)
  beta_3 >= 0 (from n=6)
  beta_4 >= 0 (from n=8)
  beta_5 >= 0 (from n=8)

Euler characteristic of path homology complex:
  chi = sum_{k>=0} (-1)^k beta_k = beta_0 - beta_1 + 0 - beta_3 + beta_4 - beta_5 + ...

  Name                   n  Betti numbers          chi     Q(chi)             rapidity
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  Transitive T_3         3  [1, 0]                   1        inf                  inf
  Cyclic C_3             3  [1, 1]                   0     1.0000             0.000000
  Generic n=5            5  [1, 2, 0, 0]            -1     0.0000     -inf+i*0.5000*pi
  Regular n=5            5  [1, 4, 0, 0]            -3    -0.5000  -0.3466+i*0.5000*pi
  Generic n=7            7  [1, 8, 0, 2]            -9    -0.8000  -0.1116+i*0.5000*pi
  Paley T_7              7  [1, 14, 0, 0]          -13    -0.8571  -0.0771+i*0.5000*pi
  n=8 with b4            8  [1, 10, 0, 1, 1]        -9    -0.8000  -0.1116+i*0.5000*pi
  n=8 generic            8  [1, 12, 0, 0, 0]       -11    -0.8333  -0.0912+i*0.5000*pi

  OBSERVATIONS:
  1. chi(path complex) = beta_0 - beta_1 - beta_3 + beta_4 - ...
     (beta_2 = 0 always, so it drops out)

  2. For transitive tournaments: beta_k = 0 for k >= 1 (acyclic).
     chi = 1. Q(1) = inf. rapidity = +inf.
     Transitive = INFINITE RAPIDITY (unreachable by continuous deformation).

  3. For cyclic C_3: beta = [1,1], chi = 0. rapidity = 0.
     The 3-cycle is the ZERO of rapidity, like the torus in surfaces!

  4. Increasing beta_1 (more independent cycles) DECREASES chi,
     which for chi < 0 makes rapidity complex.

  RAPIDITY OF INDIVIDUAL BETTI NUMBERS:
  Map each beta_k to rapidity_from_integer(beta_k):

  Transitive T (beta = [1,0,0,...]):
    rap(beta_0=1) = +inf, rap(beta_k=0 for k>0) = 0
    Total rapidity 'profile': [inf, 0, 0, ...]

  Transitive T_3      : betti=[1, 0]               -> rap = [inf, 0]
  Cyclic C_3          : betti=[1, 1]               -> rap = [inf, inf]
  Generic n=5         : betti=[1, 2, 0, 0]         -> rap = [inf, 0.549+i*pi/2, 0, 0]
  Regular n=5         : betti=[1, 4, 0, 0]         -> rap = [inf, 0.255+i*pi/2, 0, 0]
  Generic n=7         : betti=[1, 8, 0, 2]         -> rap = [inf, 0.126+i*pi/2, 0, 0.549+i*pi/2]
  Paley T_7           : betti=[1, 14, 0, 0]        -> rap = [inf, 0.072+i*pi/2, 0, 0]
  n=8 with b4         : betti=[1, 10, 0, 1, 1]     -> rap = [inf, 0.100+i*pi/2, 0, inf, inf]
  n=8 generic         : betti=[1, 12, 0, 0, 0]     -> rap = [inf, 0.084+i*pi/2, 0, 0, 0]

  PATTERN: beta_k = 0 -> rapidity 0 (absent)
           beta_k = 1 -> rapidity +inf (unique class)
           beta_k >= 2 -> complex rapidity (multiple classes, 'superluminal')

  The beta_2 = 0 theorem means: rapidity of beta_2 is ALWAYS 0.
  This is the fixed zero of the path homology rapidity profile.

  POINCARE POLYNOMIAL IN RAPIDITY:
  P(t) = sum beta_k * t^k is the Poincare polynomial.
  Evaluate at t = -1: P(-1) = chi.
  Evaluate at t = Q(-1) = 0: P(0) = beta_0.
  Evaluate at t = Q(0) = 1: P(1) = sum beta_k = total Betti number.

  Q maps evaluation points:
    t = -1 (chi) -> Q(-1) = 0
    t = 0 (beta_0) -> Q(0) = 1
    t = 1 (total) -> Q(1) = inf

  In the Q-transformed variable u = Q(t):
    P at u=0 gives chi
    P at u=1 gives beta_0
    P at u=inf gives total Betti

  This is a natural hierarchy: chi (global) -> beta_0 (connected) -> total (all)

========================================================================
SECTION 7: COVERING SPACES AND RAPIDITY OF DEGREE
========================================================================

A covering space p: Y -> X of degree n means each point has n preimages.
The fundamental group: [pi_1(X) : p_*(pi_1(Y))] = n.

Rapidity of covering degree:
  n = 1: trivial cover, rapidity = arctanh(1) = +inf  (wait, this is wrong)
  We need f(n) = (n+1)/n for the rapidity homomorphism.

  f(n) = (n+1)/n:  f(1)=2, f(2)=3/2, f(3)=4/3, ...
  rapidity(f(n)) = arctanh((n+1)/n) -- but (n+1)/n > 1 for all n >= 1!
  So this is always complex.

  Alternative: use ln(Q(n))/2 directly.

COVERING DEGREE -> RAPIDITY TABLE:
    Degree n         Q(n)                 rapidity                        Example
  ------------------------------------------------------------
           1          inf                      inf                   trivial cover
           2      -3.0000        0.549306 + i*pi/2       double cover (orientable)
           3      -2.0000        0.346574 + i*pi/2                    triple cover
           4      -1.6667        0.255413 + i*pi/2                    4-fold cover
           5      -1.5000        0.202733 + i*pi/2                    5-fold cover
           6      -1.4000        0.168236 + i*pi/2                    6-fold cover
           7      -1.3333        0.143841 + i*pi/2                    7-fold cover
          12      -1.1818        0.083527 + i*pi/2              dodecahedral cover
          24      -1.0870        0.041691 + i*pi/2  orientation double of genus-12
          60      -1.0339        0.016668 + i*pi/2               icosahedral cover

  ALL covering degrees >= 2 have Q(n) < 0, hence complex rapidity
  with imaginary part exactly pi/2.

  Real part: Re(rapidity(n)) = ln((n+1)/(n-1))/2
    n= 2: Re = ln(3/1)/2 = 0.549306
    n= 3: Re = ln(4/2)/2 = 0.346574
    n= 4: Re = ln(5/3)/2 = 0.255413
    n= 5: Re = ln(6/4)/2 = 0.202733
    n= 6: Re = ln(7/5)/2 = 0.168236
    n= 7: Re = ln(8/6)/2 = 0.143841
    n= 8: Re = ln(9/7)/2 = 0.125657
    n= 9: Re = ln(10/8)/2 = 0.111572
    n=10: Re = ln(11/9)/2 = 0.100335

  The real parts DECREASE monotonically: higher degree covers have
  rapidity closer to i*pi/2 (the infinite-degree limit).

  UNIVERSAL COVER (n = inf): rapidity -> 0 + i*pi/2 = i*pi/2.
  This matches the infinite-genus limit from Section 1!

  MULTIPLICATIVITY OF COVERING DEGREE:
  If Y -> X has degree m and Z -> Y has degree n, then Z -> X has degree m*n.
  In Q-space: Q(m*n) != Q(m)*Q(n) in general. But in rapidity:

  rapidity(m*n) = ln(Q(m*n))/2 = ln((1+mn)/(1-mn))/2
  rapidity(m) + rapidity(n) = ln((1+m)/(1-m))/2 + ln((1+n)/(1-n))/2
                            = ln((1+m)(1+n)/((1-m)(1-n)))/2

  These are NOT equal. Covering degree is multiplicative,
  but rapidity is NOT additive under multiplication of degrees.

  However: ln(m*n) = ln(m) + ln(n). The LOGARITHM of degree IS additive.
  And ln(n)/2 is the real part of rapidity for large n:
    rapidity(n) ~ ln(n)/2 + i*pi/2 as n -> inf.
  So for large covering degrees, the real part is approximately additive!

    deg 2*3=6: Re(rap(6))=0.168236, Re(rap(2))+Re(rap(3))=0.895880, ln(6)/2=0.895880
    deg 2*5=10: Re(rap(10))=0.100335, Re(rap(2))+Re(rap(5))=0.752039, ln(10)/2=1.151293
    deg 3*4=12: Re(rap(12))=0.083527, Re(rap(3))+Re(rap(4))=0.601986, ln(12)/2=1.242453
    deg 5*12=60: Re(rap(60))=0.016668, Re(rap(5))+Re(rap(12))=0.286260, ln(60)/2=2.047172

  The approximation Re(rapidity(n)) ~ ln(n)/2 works well for moderate n.
  This means: rapidity of covering degree is APPROXIMATELY ADDITIVE
  under composition of covers, with the approximation improving for larger degrees.

  EULER CHARACTERISTIC UNDER COVERING:
  chi(Y) = n * chi(X) for an n-fold cover Y -> X.

  Example: double cover of genus-2 surface (chi=-2):
    chi(cover) = 2 * (-2) = -4 => genus-3 surface
    Q(chi=-2) = -0.3333, Q(chi=-4) = -0.6000
    Ratio Q(-4)/Q(-2) = 1.800000
    Compare Q(2) = -3.0000

  In general: chi(cover)/chi(base) = n, but
  Q(n*chi) / Q(chi) is NOT simply Q(n).
  The rapidity encoding is nonlinear: it captures the topology
  in a different way than the additive structure of chi.

========================================================================
SECTION 8: MOBIUS STRIP AS OCTAVE SHIFT
========================================================================

The Mobius strip M and its double cover (the cylinder/annulus C).

  Mobius strip: chi(M) = 0  (non-orientable, with boundary)
  Cylinder:     chi(C) = 0  (orientable, with boundary)
  Covering degree: 2

  Both have chi = 0, so rapidity(chi) = 0 for both.
  The covering degree 2 has rapidity:
    rapidity(2) = (0.5493061443340549+1.5707963267948966j)
    = ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2
    Real part: ln(3)/2 = 0.549306

  OCTAVE INTERPRETATION:
  In music, an octave = frequency ratio of 2:1.
  rapidity(2) = ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2.

  If we use the LOGARITHMIC rapidity (just ln(n)/2):
    ln(2)/2 = 0.346574

  In the f(n)=(n+1)/n framework:
    f(2) = 3/2, arctanh(3/2) = complex
    ln(Q(2))/2 = ln(-3)/2 = ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2

  The 'octave' in rapidity space is the complex number ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2.
  The Mobius strip IS one octave (degree-2 cover) away from its double cover.

  MORE PRECISELY:
  The cylinder C is a 2:1 cover of the Mobius strip M.
  Both have the same Euler characteristic (chi = 0).
  The topological difference is ENTIRELY in the covering structure.
  This covering structure lives at rapidity(2) = ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2.

  The imaginary part pi/2 reflects the SIGN FLIP in orientation
  (the Mobius strip reverses orientation, contributing a phase of pi).
  The half-phase pi/2 in rapidity corresponds to the half-twist.

  OTHER NON-ORIENTABLE SURFACES AND THEIR DOUBLE COVERS:
  Surface                chi Double cover         chi_dbl Degree
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  RP^2                     1 S^2                        2      2
  Klein bottle             0 Torus                      0      2
  Genus-3 nonor.          -1 Genus-2 orient.           -2      2
  Genus-4 nonor.          -2 Genus-3 orient.           -4      2
  Genus-k nonor.         2-k Genus-(k-1) or.       2(2-k)      2

  NOTE: For RP^2, chi(RP^2) = 1, chi(S^2) = 2.
    chi(double cover) = 2 * chi(base) for orientable double cover:
    chi(S^2) = 2 * chi(RP^2) = 2*1 = 2. Correct!

  rapidity(chi=1) = arctanh(1) = +inf.
  RP^2 is at INFINITE rapidity (like the transitive tournament).
  Its double cover S^2 has chi=2, rapidity = ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2.

  THE OCTAVE LADDER OF TOPOLOGY:
  Each 2:1 covering adds one 'octave' in the rapidity of the covering space.

  Start with S^1 (circle), chi = 0, rapidity = 0.
  2:1 cover of S^1 is S^1 again (z -> z^2). chi still 0.
  But the covering NUMBER accumulates:

    2^1 =    2-fold cover: rapidity = 0.549306 + i*pi/2, ln(2^1)/2 = 0.346574
    2^2 =    4-fold cover: rapidity = 0.255413 + i*pi/2, ln(2^2)/2 = 0.693147
    2^3 =    8-fold cover: rapidity = 0.125657 + i*pi/2, ln(2^3)/2 = 1.039721
    2^4 =   16-fold cover: rapidity = 0.062582 + i*pi/2, ln(2^4)/2 = 1.386294
    2^5 =   32-fold cover: rapidity = 0.031260 + i*pi/2, ln(2^5)/2 = 1.732868
    2^6 =   64-fold cover: rapidity = 0.015626 + i*pi/2, ln(2^6)/2 = 2.079442
    2^7 =  128-fold cover: rapidity = 0.007813 + i*pi/2, ln(2^7)/2 = 2.426015
    2^8 =  256-fold cover: rapidity = 0.003906 + i*pi/2, ln(2^8)/2 = 2.772589

  The real part of rapidity for 2^k-fold covers:
    ln((2^k+1)/(2^k-1))/2 -> ln(1)/2 = 0 as k -> inf

  But ln(2^k)/2 = k*ln(2)/2, which grows linearly!
  The two formulas diverge: rapidity_from_integer gives the 'intrinsic' rapidity
  (which saturates), while ln(n)/2 gives the 'logarithmic' rapidity (which grows).

  RESOLUTION: The rapidity_from_integer formula maps n -> arctanh(n),
  which for n > 1 is complex. The real part converges to 0 as n -> inf.
  This reflects that VERY LARGE covering degrees are all 'essentially the same'
  from the rapidity perspective -- they are all 'superluminal'.

  The Mobius octave: the degree-2 covering is the SMALLEST nontrivial cover,
  and it is the one that distinguishes orientable from non-orientable.
  Its rapidity ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2 = 0.549306 + 1.570796i
  is the fundamental unit of the 'topological octave'.

========================================================================
SYNTHESIS: RAPIDITY STRUCTURE OF TOPOLOGY
========================================================================

KEY FINDINGS:

1. EULER CHARACTERISTIC: The torus (chi=0) is the rapidity ZERO.
   Positive chi (sphere, RP^2) gives complex rapidity with Im = pi/2.
   Negative chi (higher genus) also gives complex rapidity with Im = pi/2.
   The chi -> rapidity map is nonlinear but respects the torus as identity.

2. GAUSS-BONNET: Curvature integral = 2*pi*chi, so curvature rapidity
   is just a rescaled chi-rapidity. Zero curvature <-> zero rapidity.

3. POINCARE DISK: The rapidity phi = arctanh(|z|) gives the metric
   ds^2 = 4*d(phi)^2 + sinh^2(2*phi)*d(theta)^2.
   Hyperbolic distance = 2 * rapidity. Q(|z|) = exp(hyperbolic distance).
   This is the DEEPEST connection: rapidity IS half the hyperbolic distance.

4. KNOT INVARIANTS: Q maps roots of unity to the imaginary axis:
   Q(e^{2pi*i/n}) = i*cot(pi/n). Jones polynomial at Cayley-transformed
   roots probes the 'split real form' of quantum groups.

5. HOPF FIBRATION: Q transforms the Hopf map z_1/z_2 to (z_1+z_2)/(z_2-z_1).
   The equator of S^2 maps to the imaginary axis (= rapidity axis).
   The Hopf invariant 1 lives at rapidity +infinity (arctanh(1)).

6. TOURNAMENT BETTI NUMBERS: Transitive tournament has chi=1 (infinite rapidity).
   The 3-cycle has chi=0 (zero rapidity, like the torus).
   beta_2=0 universally means the degree-2 rapidity is ALWAYS zero.
   Higher Betti numbers give complex rapidity (superluminal).

7. COVERING SPACES: All degrees >= 2 have complex rapidity with Im = pi/2.
   Real part ~ ln(n)/2 for large n (approximately additive under composition).
   The universal cover (n=inf) has rapidity i*pi/2 (purely imaginary).

8. MOBIUS STRIP: The degree-2 double cover has rapidity ln(3)/2 + i*pi/2.
   This is the 'topological octave' -- the fundamental covering unit.
   The imaginary pi/2 corresponds to the orientation-reversing half-twist.

UNIFYING THEME: Rapidity provides a natural 'relativistic' coordinate system
for topology. The torus/3-cycle is the rest frame (rapidity 0). Positive and
negative curvature are 'boosts' in opposite directions. Covering spaces are
'superluminal' (complex rapidity). The Poincare disk metric in rapidity
coordinates is the most concrete instance: hyperbolic distance = 2 * rapidity.

